This program implements a Hamiltonian-informed Generative Adversarial Network (GAN) to model and generate time-optimal trajectories for the Zermelo Navigation problem.
The generator is trained not only to fool the discriminator but also to minimize a physics-based loss computed from the system's Hamiltonian, ensuring physical consistency in the generated trajectories.
The training data consists of 4000 expert trajectories in a 7D state space. 
The generator maps 20-dimensional latent vectors to 175-dimensional state trajectories, while the discriminator evaluates the realism of partial trajectory representations. 
The training process incorporates both adversarial loss (via MSE between real/fake labels) and a Hamiltonian regularization term to penalize physically invalid trajectories.
After training, the model generates 1000 synthetic samples that emulate the statistical and physical properties of the true data.
The outputs, as well as the trained model weights, are saved for downstream analysis or control applications.
